        "original_id": "0cc15a27-036d-5df5-915d-5a6e5fde3370",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- People: Louis le Brocquy, King Conchobar, Cúchulainn, Queen Medb, Mary Colum\n- Places: Ulster, Connacht, Ireland, Lowland Scotland\n- Works: Táin illustrations\n- Languages: Scots, Ulster Scots, Irish\n- Topics: Táin, Brown Bull of Cuailgne\n\nFacts:\n- The Táin illustrations by Louis le Brocquy depict the exploits of King Conchobar and his chief warrior Cúchulainn, who is called \"The Hound of Ulster.\"\n- The Táin tells of Queen Medb of Connacht invading Ulster in an attempt to capture the Brown Bull of Cuailgne.\n- Ulster is a province in the north of the island of Ireland.\n- Scots is a Germanic language variety spoken in Lowland Scotland and in parts of Ulster, where the local dialect is called Ulster Scots.\n- Mary Colum, née Maguire, was born on June 14, 1884 and died on October 22, 1957.\n- Mary Colum was an Irish literary critic and author who also co-founded a literary journal.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
        "id": 1,
        "original_id": "2a9af0c1-63d5-5cb6-89dd-4d56d315b357",
        "text": "Page: Louis le Brocquy Táin illustrations. Sentence: It tells of the exploits of King Conchobar and his chief warrior Cúchulainn (\"The Hound of Ulster\") and of the invasion of Ulster by Queen Medb of Connacht in an attempt to capture the Brown Bull of Cuailgne.\nPage: Ulster. Sentence: Ulster ( ; Irish: \"Ulaidh\" ] or \"Cúige Uladh\" ] , Ulster Scots: \"Ulstèr\" or \"Ulster\") is a province in the north of the island of Ireland.\nPage: Scots language. Sentence: Scots is the Germanic language variety spoken in Lowland Scotland and parts of Ulster (where the local dialect is known as Ulster Scots).\nPage: Mary Colum. Sentence: Mary Colum (née Maguire, June 14, 1884 – October 22, 1957) was an Irish literary critic and author, who also co-founded a literary journal.",
